WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.262 --> 00:00:04.160
OLI PALL GEIRSSON: Iceland can&nbsp;
be harsh like a lunar landscape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.280
But it can also be vivid and green.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:19.120
And I have these moments when I'm biking to&nbsp;
work and it's dark. And I can see the stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:28.800
and I can see the moon. A harsh winter and a&nbsp;
long night can also be beautiful. And when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.680
summer finally arrives, it brings out the&nbsp;
joy. And it really, really reconnect to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:43.840
people who live here again, a really close-knitted&nbsp;
community. And being a part of that matters to me.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.600
CREW:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.120
Sir, can you just introduce yourself?

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.840
- Yes. My name is Oli Pall&nbsp;
Geirsson. And I serve as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.840
chief data officer at the City of Reykjavik.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:04.800
OLI PALL GEIRSSON:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.000
The data scientist finds the story in the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:22.400
When I started here, I felt like it was a clean&nbsp;
slate. Our mission was to build something. We know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.720
that digital transformation is ahead. We need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:35.280
evolve with society. How can we, as&nbsp;
a city, use data and data analytics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:44.560
to help people, to have them accessible on the&nbsp;
web, to put down these barriers, these walls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:53.840
to improve the quality of life&nbsp;
for the residents of Reykjavik?

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.760
Your work means something to somebody.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:09.920
Our role here as data science office is to&nbsp;
bring value from data to our stakeholders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:16.320
within the City of Reykjavik. The stakeholders&nbsp;
could be the Department of School and Leisure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:22.880
It could be the Department of Welfare, Urban&nbsp;
Planning, or City Council, or the residents.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.200
- It's almost like going through the phone&nbsp;
book or something like that. But you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:35.840
go there, talk to them, listen. And&nbsp;
do it again, and again, and again.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:44.240
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: We are just right outside the&nbsp;
Arctic Circle, so during the winter, it gets dark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:52.640
And it's obviously cold in Iceland.&nbsp;
So the first thing you kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.680
get to know about Icelanders is that they&nbsp;
are crazy about their swimming pools.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.109
[SPLASHING]

00:03:01.109 --> 00:03:05.680
- It's ingrained in our culture.&nbsp;
When you have family photos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.480
you maybe go to the countryside and take&nbsp;
photos. But my brother, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.920
got a photographer to meet them up at the swimming&nbsp;
pool, because that's where they were every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:20.720
essentially. So his family photos, you&nbsp;
know, they're standing by the swimming pool.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:37.200
- I got a frequent request, even phone calls,&nbsp;
on why can't I see how many guests are in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.200
swimming pools right now. I need to know this. And&nbsp;
also like why can't I just predict how many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:49.360
are going to be in the pools for next hour or so,&nbsp;
two hours or more, in different swimming pools.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.920
And what we would like to do is to show the&nbsp;
percentage, the capacity of the swimming pool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:02.080
Like Laugardalslaug, which is one of the cool&nbsp;
swimming pools here, is at 50% right now. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:09.120
we predict it's going to be at 70% in an hour and&nbsp;
35% in two hours. So we started looking into this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.720
and this became a project on our side&nbsp;
with the Department of Sports and Leisure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:20.360
But when we started to dive into it, we saw&nbsp;
that, hey, this is going to be a bit complicated.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.880
- I should be looking at you and not the camera.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:23.200
CREW: Yeah.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:23.600
- OK.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:24.560
- Should I start now?

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:25.200
CREW: Yes.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.720
- I'm talking to you. OK.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.320
CREW: Who the heck are you?

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:36.000
- If I knew-- no, I'm kidding. My&nbsp;
name is Inga Ros Gunnarsdottir.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.179
- My name is Hlynur Hallgrimsson.

00:04:39.179 --> 00:04:39.643
- My name is Sigridur Erla.

00:04:39.643 --> 00:04:40.640
- My name is Thorbjorn Thorarinsson.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.240
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: I'm a senior data scientist.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.000
SIGRIDUR ERLA FRIDGEIRSDOTTIR:&nbsp;
I am a data scientist.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:44.880
THORBJORN THORARINSSON: Data scientist.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.960
INGA ROS GUNNARSDOTTIR: A project&nbsp;
manager for Reykjavik City

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:47.520
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: Reykjavik.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.400
SIGRIDUR ERLA FRIDGEIRSDOTTIR: Reykjavik City.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.360
THORBJORN THORARINSSON: The City of Reykjavik.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.920
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: My role is&nbsp;
modeling, advanced analytics, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.320
predictive modeling. That's me, I guess.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.040
THORBJORN THORARINSSON: Finding meaning or value,&nbsp;
and something that looks like chaos at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.400
and finding a meaningful and&nbsp;
beautiful way to present it.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.880
INGA ROS GUNNARSDOTTIR: To put a structure&nbsp;
on the data team's work, how to break it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:15.680
down and help them keep their deadlines,&nbsp;
or nothing ever happens too quickly for me.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.040
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: We have&nbsp;
different backgrounds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:25.600
different skill sets within the team.&nbsp;
Thorb is an amazing machine learning guru,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:33.640
but he is also-- like he has this eye for&nbsp;
detail. There's something of an artist about him.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.880
- Hlynur, he is a great programmer.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:41.360
CREW: And what happens when your&nbsp;
team ignores your deadlines?

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.480
- They never do.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.280
-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.320
We have these gates, and it counts how&nbsp;
many guests are arriving into the pool.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:00.880
- We can say, OK, this is just a cumulative&nbsp;
sum of people that have gone swimming today.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.800
- That's a bit of a pickle because we&nbsp;
really don't want to know how many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.280
have been counted in. Hey, there are 10,000&nbsp;
people on the pool. That makes no sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:17.520
Can we fix that without buying new gates&nbsp;
or changing the protocol? Turned out that--

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:23.360
- --we have historical data&nbsp;
for in and out of the gates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.280
for one swimming pool in Iceland,&nbsp;
the largest swimming pool.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.200
- It's because there you have these bracelets&nbsp;
that you need to hand in when you go out.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.480
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: So we contacted the&nbsp;
hardware provider and they provided us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.160
with historical data.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:43.040
OLI PALL GEIRSSON: And the data is corrected,&nbsp;
with respect to these bracelets, and afterward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.160
So we have the data on how long they're staying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.000
There is a distributional property&nbsp;
that we can extract out of it.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.320
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: But to make&nbsp;
things a little bit more complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:58.080
if there's a queue of people&nbsp;
trying to leave the swimming pool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:05.360
the people who work at the swimming pools are kind&nbsp;
enough to like, OK we'll just open the gates for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:11.280
you. You don't have to use your card to leave. But&nbsp;
that's wonderful for everybody except for the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:19.200
people, because that ruins our data, essentially.&nbsp;
We look at it like it's the kindness of man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:29.840
ruining our perfect structured data. So&nbsp;
we have to account for that discrepancy.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:37.520
- Our model is a very simple one. It's a&nbsp;
linear plans model and the only inputs are--

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:38.400
OLI PALL GEIRSSON: --the time of the day,

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.520
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: --day of the week,

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:40.480
OLI PALL GEIRSSON: --and times a year.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:46.960
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: Every five minutes we&nbsp;
have a process and web methods that reads data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:53.760
from the swimming pool gate and stores them in&nbsp;
our data link. We then use the pins packets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:04.080
We get pinned linear models from RStudio&nbsp;
Connect, and we then predict the duration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:12.400
then we pin that result staple in RStudio&nbsp;
Connect. And that's what our Shiny app accesses.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:17.200
OLI PALL GEIRSSON: So before launching&nbsp;
it, it had to go through testing--

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.720
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: --to make sure&nbsp;
that this is grounded in reality.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:25.360
- Because we are not swimming pool executives.&nbsp;
We don't really know these numbers.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.680
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: We set up a separate Shiny&nbsp;
app for the managers of the swimming pools.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:36.880
- So going through the testing, it matters to&nbsp;
it to go fast, to deliver quickly, and in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:43.120
that they understand. To show them the numbers,&nbsp;
show them the time series, and preferably life.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:48.080
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: So it's just like an&nbsp;
interactive GT plot. So you just hover over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:53.680
like this is the time now, it's half past 12:00.&nbsp;
The app says that there are 50 people here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.320 --> 00:09:00.640
Let's do a count. The feedback was like it's&nbsp;
essentially, 10 minutes later, everything is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.960
spot on. And we thought, OK is there something we&nbsp;
need to improve? And they said, no, this is fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:10.400
because that's maybe how long it takes for you&nbsp;
to walk there, or bike, or ride your car there.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:12.880
OLI PALL GEIRSSON: When we&nbsp;
launched it, it was immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:15.200
well-received by the residents of Reykjavik.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:20.640
-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:23.120
Like a lot of how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.880
are able to get things off the ground&nbsp;
quickly has to do with R and RStudio Connect.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:33.280
THORBJORN THORARINSSON: So&nbsp;
when you do it often enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:39.360
you have all the tools and you just&nbsp;
have to pick and choose what works best.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.640
HLYNUR HALLGRIMSSON: We are the people&nbsp;
that actually work with the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:44.720
but now we're also the people that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:50.160
deploy this, and schedule it, and put up&nbsp;
a Shiny app in production within a week.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.760
- All the process of transforming and&nbsp;
building up this analytical tables of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.000
products and publishing it, the data science team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:07.680
if it can do all of that inside one domain,&nbsp;
they become superheroes. They can do everything.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:12.800
THORBJORN THORARINSSON:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:20.160
I think I do what I do not only because I&nbsp;
love coding and making beautiful things, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:26.240
also because I believe that working&nbsp;
at the City of Reykjavik, I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:31.840
improve people's lives if only a small bit.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:38.080
INGA ROS GUNNARSDOTTIR: Making a difference,&nbsp;
and maybe it's small, at least it's making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:46.480
an impact which hopefully ends with the&nbsp;
citizens and making life better for them.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:53.040
OLI PALL GEIRSSON: If you want to&nbsp;
make changes, if you want to matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:59.280
then you need to embrace the fact it's going to be&nbsp;
challenging. You have to just know it's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:05.920
be ride. That's something that I didn't quite&nbsp;
realize the magnitude of it before I started.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:27.840
But that's a ride worth taking, because&nbsp;
through the experience, you grow.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:40.844
[MUSIC PLAYING]

